speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I should like to ask a question of the Secretary of State for External Affairs, of which I have not had a chance to give him notice. Has the minister's attention been drawn to the Ottawa Journal of yesterday, which contains a report of a speech by General McNaughton in Vancouver to the Junior Chamber of Commerce, in which, speaking of the Russians, he is reported to have said;
They will turn to war as a means of achieving their ends.
At a time when everyone is advocating more moderate statements, does the minister think that someone who bears such a close relationship to public affairs and the government of this country should make a statement of that kind?

First, Mr. Chairman, I would agree with the hon. member for Sudbury that the film is a tremendous educational factor and its use should be extended. I should like to ask the minister if he could not get a crew of camera people to go up into the Peace river district and there make a film showing the resources of the country, the land that is available for settlement, and the development that is going on, particularly the oil development. Just the other day at Whitelaw a well was brought in which is running six hundred barrels of oil per day. I doubt if on that country the film board has a film of the character which I outlined.
The Alaska highway is also a subject which should be filmed. Those films should be sent broadcast throughout America because the highway opens up a great tourist area there as well as an industrial area. The scenic features of the Rocky mountains, which are cut in three places by the Alaska highway, are superior to those in Switzerland;

Supply-Resources and Development yet little has been done to publicize that fact. The possibilities of the Yukon as a place for tourists and also for industrial development could be shown only by film. Some time ago I saw a film on the Peace river country made by the national film board. It was dated 1941. In the last ten years so many things have happened north of Edmonton that there has been a transformation of the entire country, and a film dated 1941 is not particularly useful in presenting the story as it is in 1950. I would ask the minister to direct his department to these parts of Canada which should be illustrated by the film board.
